<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Owain Cyfeiliog</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130–1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Owain Cyfeiliog was a Welsh prince who took the southern part of Powys after the death of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a> in 1160. He often sided with the English crown against Gwynedd and was also a poet. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strata-marcella-abbey">Strata Marcella Abbey</a>, and his son Gwenwynwyn gave the principality its name, Powys Wenwynwyn.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain ap Gruffudd</a> was a son of Gruffudd ap Maredudd and a grandson of Maredudd ap Bleddyn. He took the byname Cyfeiliog to set him apart from the other Owain ap Gruffudd of his generation, Owain Gwynedd, whose daughter Gwenllian he married. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madog-ap-maredudd">Madog ap Maredudd</a>, the last king of Powys, gave him the cantref of Cyfeiliog to rule in 1147, and on Madog's death most of southern Powys passed to Owain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1165 he joined the other Welsh princes in resisting the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, but afterwards he usually supported the English crown. In 1170 he gave land for the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strata-marcella-abbey">Strata Marcella Abbey</a>. In 1188 he refused to meet or support Baldwin, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, and Giraldus Cambrensis when they travelled around Wales raising men for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and he was excommunicated for it. He also appears in the romance Fouke le Fitz Waryn as a knight who strikes Fulk with a spear.</p><p>In 1195 he handed most of his lands to his son Gwenwynwyn and retired to Strata Marcella, where he died and was buried two years later. His illegitimate son Cadwallon received the commotes of Llannerch Hudol and Broniarth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Only one poem ascribed to Owain survives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirlas-owain">Hirlas Owain</a>, and it is commonly rated among the finest Welsh poems of its age. It is set at his court after a raid in 1155 to free his brother Meurig from prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelor">Maelor</a>: Owain calls for the drinking horn to pass to each man of his bodyguard in turn, praising each, and grieves for two who fell. Its most recent editor, Gruffydd Aled Williams, has suggested that it was in fact written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynddelw-brydydd-mawr">Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr</a>, the leading court poet of the century, perhaps with Owain's collaboration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Owain Cyfeiliog” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century Madog ap Maredudd patronised Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr, one of the greatest court poets of medieval Wales, and <b>Owain Cyfeiliog</b>, Madog’s nephew and ruler of southern Powys, was himself a poet to whom the poem Hirlas Owain is attributed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After Madog ap Maredudd's death in 1160, his nephew <b>Owain Cyfeiliog</b> took the southern part of Powys.</blockquote><a href="/state/powys-wenwynwyn">Powys Wenwynwyn</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=1173" aria-label="Wales on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Owain Cyfeiliog"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4810 5647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4810 5647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.35,-3.63&amp;year=1173">What was here in 1173? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
